"""Concept lessons in syllabus order. The ¶ marker separates teaching paragraphs."""
from teaching.foundations import put

put(5, '''Imagine one payment class that checks an order, creates a client for a payment provider's software library (SDK), calculates fees, and writes audit records. A fee change and a provider change both require editing it. Let us separate the work, beginning with what the application needs from a provider. ¶ The application wants to request a charge and receive an outcome. It need not know the provider's JSON field names or connection setup. A gateway offers an operation such as charge(command); a provider adapter translates that request into the provider's protocol. Presenting the useful operation while hiding those implementation details is abstraction. The operation's contract must still explain what its result means. ¶ Now consider the payment's own state. Suppose any caller can set payment.status to “paid.” It can bypass the check that a charge actually succeeded. Encapsulation controls access to internal state so that changes pass through operations that enforce the rules. An operation that records verified approval can check the current state before allowing the transition. Hiding a field helps only if the permitted operations preserve the rule. ¶ We may have two provider adapters offering the same charge operation. The payment service can call either through the gateway contract, without choosing a different calling procedure for each provider. This ability to use different implementations through a common contract is polymorphism. Both implementations must preserve the meanings callers rely on, including the distinction between decline and uncertainty. ¶ Inheritance creates a specialised class from a base class, reusing or overriding its behaviour. It can support polymorphism when a subclass fulfils the base class's contract, but it is not required for abstraction, encapsulation, or polymorphism. Composed objects and functions can also provide those capabilities. Keep these questions separate: what operation is exposed, who may change the state, which implementations callers can use, and whether a class hierarchy helps express that relationship. || PaymentService handles intent / Provider contract hides SDK / Payment state protects transitions / Audit records observable outcomes

Suppose callers use an Account contract that permits withdrawal when sufficient funds are available. A ReadOnlyAccount subclass that always rejects withdrawal changes an assumption those callers rely on. Sharing balance fields through inheritance has not established a valid behavioural relationship. A subclass offered in place of its parent must preserve the parent's contract, including the operations callers are allowed to request. ¶ Composition builds behaviour by connecting collaborators. A payment service can receive a fraud policy and a gateway, then call each for its own part of the work. To change the fraud policy, we supply another policy while keeping the gateway. To change providers, we replace the gateway while keeping the policy. Inheritance that combines both choices can lead to a subclass for every policy–provider combination. ¶ The inheritance-versus-composition figure shows the same issue with content and delivery channels: two independent choices need not become one growing hierarchy. Composition adds wiring and contracts, so use it where those variations are real. Inheritance remains useful when the specialised object consistently fulfils the inherited contract. Judge that relationship by what callers may do, rather than by similar fields or convenient code reuse. || Subclass combines unrelated changes / Policy and gateway vary independently / Compose collaborators at startup / Test behavior at each contract

What does charge(100) mean? It might mean one hundred dollars, one dollar expressed in cents, or another currency entirely. An interface describes the operations available to callers. Its contract must also explain their inputs, outcomes, and failure behaviour. ¶ A clearer operation might accept amount_minor=100, currency=USD, and operation_id=P7. The amount and currency identify one dollar in this representation; the operation identifier names this intended payment. The contract must explain how repeated use of that identifier is handled. Merely passing an identifier does not make a provider recognise duplicates. ¶ The result also needs a precise meaning. “Accepted for processing” leaves different work for the caller than “captured.” A payment may be approved, declined, or uncertain, with a provider reference when available. A timeout after submission is uncertain because the provider may already have charged. The caller needs a supported way to obtain further evidence before deciding what to do next. ¶ This behavioural contract lets the application replace adapters without silently changing payment meaning. Test the real adapter as well as a substitute: a fake returning “approved” cannot establish how the provider handles lost replies or repeated operations. || Command: 100 minor units in USD / Stable operation identity supplied / Outcome: approved, declined or uncertain / Transport failure is not business decline

A fee rule belongs with related payment rules. An SMS receipt formatter has a different reason to change. If adding SMS requires editing ledger arithmetic, the responsibilities are entangled. Move receipt formatting into a formatter so that a new layout has a clear place to change, while fee calculations remain with the fee policy. ¶ Cohesion describes how closely the work inside a module belongs together. Coupling describes its dependencies on other modules. A payment module still needs a provider, but depending on a narrow gateway contract requires less knowledge than constructing provider requests throughout its code. Provider field names can then change inside the adapter. ¶ Test the arrangement with likely edits: where would a new receipt format, fee rule, or provider go? The cohesion/coupling figure follows these changes to their owners. Some changes still cross boundaries: adding a new payment outcome may require both the adapter and its callers to change. Avoid dividing work so finely that a simple action becomes difficult to follow. Extract a module when it protects a rule, hides changeable details, or gives a real change a clear home. || Fee policy changes / Provider schema stays unchanged / Receipt adapter changes / Ledger invariant stays unchanged

Suppose we add a second payment provider. Which code should change, and what must remain true for callers? SOLID gives us five related principles for examining this change. Each asks a different question about responsibilities and dependencies. ¶ Single responsibility asks whether a module has a focused reason to change. Provider request formatting and fee policy can change independently, so give each a clear owner. This does not require a separate class for every method; several operations may serve the same responsibility. ¶ Open/closed asks whether a useful variation can be added through an established extension point. If the service already uses a gateway contract, we can add a provider adapter without rewriting its payment decisions. The contract itself may still need to change when the business requirements change. No design can anticipate every future variation. ¶ Liskov substitution asks whether an implementation can replace another without breaking the promised behaviour. A replacement gateway must preserve amount units and outcome meanings. Turning every timeout into “declined” would violate that expectation even if the method signature stayed identical. ¶ Interface segregation asks whether callers depend only on operations they need. A checkout caller that needs charging should not have to depend on unrelated provider-reporting operations. Dependency inversion asks which way code dependencies point: the payment policy uses an application-owned gateway contract, and provider adapters implement that contract. At runtime, the policy still calls the chosen adapter; it simply no longer needs the provider's types and error codes. ¶ These principles help judge a design, rather than prescribe an interface around every class. Apply them to a concrete change or testing problem. An elaborate extension system can cost more to understand than a direct edit when the supposed variation never occurs. || Separate reasons to change / Preserve behavioral substitution / Keep collaborator contracts narrow / Invert unstable dependency knowledge

Suppose every gateway contains a copy of the same currency-conversion policy. When that policy changes, updating only one copy makes providers calculate different amounts. This is duplicated knowledge: the concern behind DRY, “Don't Repeat Yourself.” Give the shared policy one maintained definition that the relevant callers use. ¶ Similar-looking code need not represent the same knowledge. Two forms may both reject blank text, while one checks a postal address and the other checks a product description. If their rules change independently, merging them can create a dependency between unrelated policies. Ask whether the checks must agree for the same business reason. ¶ KISS asks us to keep the required design understandable. A direct fee function may be enough; a configurable rule engine needs a stronger reason. YAGNI, “You Aren't Gonna Need It,” discourages building speculative features before there is a need. Together, these ideas suggest a practical approach: share a rule where it has one meaning and owner, and add structure when a real requirement makes that structure useful. || Similar syntax may differ in meaning / Repeated business rule has one owner / Extract only stable shared knowledge / Avoid speculative configuration layers

Consider order.customer.account.settings.currency. The caller now knows the shape of several objects. Changing the account's settings representation may force that caller to change too, even though the caller still needs exactly the same information. ¶ If the caller wants the payment currency, its contract can ask for that through order.paymentCurrency(). The object that knows the representation performs the lookup. Callers then need to understand the payment-currency operation, rather than the route through customer, account, and settings. ¶ The Law of Demeter encourages limiting such knowledge of distant collaborators. Its value here is reduced dependence on internal structure. Counting dots is not the objective: a fluent builder, whose chained calls deliberately construct a result, or a traversal of plain data can be clear. A forwarding method is useful when it hides a meaningful representation decision; adding layers of forwarding without hiding such a decision only makes the call harder to follow. Ask which details callers would otherwise have to understand and maintain. || Caller traverses internal object graph / Representation changes in one owner / Many callers break / Expose a purposeful operation instead

A caller reads an account balance, decides whether money is available, and writes a new reserved balance. It has taken responsibility for a rule that belongs with the account. Another caller may implement the rule differently. “Tell, Don't Ask” suggests requesting reserve(amount) from the owner of that rule instead. ¶ Suppose the balance is 1,000 minor units and no money is reserved. A reservation of 500 can succeed, leaving 500 available. A later reservation of 600 must fail under the rule that reserved money cannot exceed the balance. The operation gives the owner a place to check this rule and perform the change together. Reading a balance for a report remains a useful query. ¶ The method boundary organises responsibility, but simultaneous callers still need coordination. If two processes both observe 1,000 available and each reserves 600, separate checks can let both proceed. The actual shared storage boundary must make the check and update atomic: other writers must not slip a conflicting change between them. Moving the method onto an object does not supply that guarantee. Keep the business rule and the mechanism enforcing it under concurrent use explicit. || Caller requests reserve(500) / Owner checks available funds / Owner updates reserved balance / Caller receives explicit outcome

A call named payment.status() should report the payment's state. If reading status unexpectedly charges a card, drawing a screen can create a financial effect. We should be able to repeat the status query without intending to change the payment. ¶ Command-query separation distinguishes queries, which report information, from commands, which request state changes. An operation named payment.capture(operation_id) clearly requests an effect. The strict formulation keeps commands from returning values. Many practical APIs let a command return information about its effect, such as an operation identifier; document that convention while keeping the effect explicit. ¶ This separation helps a reader predict what a call can do. It does not imply that two status reads must return the same value: another operation may change the payment between them. CQRS, Command Query Responsibility Segregation, goes further by giving commands and queries different models where their needs justify it. Ordinary command-query separation does not require separate databases, services, or event histories. Begin with clear operation meanings before considering a larger architecture. || Query reads payment status / Command requests a payment / Command may return operation ID / CQRS is a separate design choice

Suppose several helpers edit the same payment summary. To understand its final value, a reader must track every mutation, or change to that value. An immutable value cannot be changed after construction. We calculate a new summary and leave the old one available for comparison. ¶ For example, let amount=100 and fee=3, both expressed in USD minor units. A calculation can return a new summary with total=103 without altering its input. A pure function depends only on its explicit inputs and produces no externally visible effects. This makes the arithmetic straightforward to check: the same inputs produce the same result without contacting a provider or database. ¶ Keep such calculations separate from effects, including provider calls and database writes. The trace shows the calculation, a new value, the gateway effect, and storage of the outcome as distinct work. The local storage transaction does not include the provider's remote charge. Immutability also has a limited scope: an immutable command can still be executed twice, and it cannot undo a charge. Execution still needs authorisation, operation identity, and duplicate handling. Measure copying and allocation costs where they matter. || Pure fee calculation / New immutable payment value / Explicit gateway side effect / Persist outcome under a transaction

Payment 42 is still the same payment after its status changes. Its identity matters, so it is an entity. Creating another payment for the same amount does not replace payment 42: the two payments have different histories. ¶ Money has a different meaning. Two Money values with the same currency and minor-unit amount can represent the same value without separate identities; they are value objects. Replacing Money(USD, 100) with another equal Money value is usually harmless. Replacing payment 42 with another payment for that amount is not. Immutable representations are useful for values, although identity and immutability answer different questions: an entity can also be represented by successive immutable snapshots with the same identifier. ¶ A domain model expresses business concepts and their rules. Money arithmetic should reject incompatible currencies unless an explicit conversion rule applies. A payment transition should preserve its required amount and currency rules. A domain service can express a business rule involving several values or entities when no single entity naturally owns it; the gateway adapter still handles provider communication. ¶ Put important rules where callers can consistently use them. Simple transport data need not become an elaborate object model, but scattering payment invariants across controllers makes them difficult to preserve. || Payment entity: ID 42 / Money value: USD 100 minor units / Transition preserves amount invariant / Domain service coordinates a business rule

During checkout, an orchestrator coordinates a sequence: it asks inventory to reserve items, asks payments to authorise, and records the order's progress. Each collaborator owns its own rules and returns an explicit result. An object collaboration describes this conversation, including responsibilities and returned information, rather than only the fields each object holds. ¶ Follow one exchange. Inventory returns a reservation identifier. Checkout records that identifier so it knows which reservation belongs to the order. If payment is definitely declined, checkout can request release of that reservation. Inventory should not quietly edit payment records, and checkout should not infer a reservation's identity from unrelated stock changes. ¶ An uncertain payment needs a different recovery path from a definite decline. Checkout must retain the payment's identity and seek evidence; it cannot assume that the charge failed. Releasing inventory, refunding, or cancelling may themselves require tracked operations. Returning an error does not undo earlier effects. ¶ Also consider where calls run. Several small in-process calls may be cheap. Moving the same conversation across a network adds waiting and partial failures, where one participant succeeds while another cannot communicate. Design a remote contract for that boundary rather than automatically copying the object interface. || Checkout requests reservation / Inventory returns reservation ID / Checkout requests authorization / Order records explicit outcomes

To test a declined payment, give the service a test gateway that returns “declined.” The test can then check the order's resulting state without contacting a real provider. Controllable inputs and visible outcomes make such tests useful. ¶ A useful test calls the payment operation and checks that the decline was recorded, the order did not become paid, and shipment was not initiated. Another test supplies an uncertain outcome and checks that the order remains pending for investigation. These checks express the behaviour the user depends on, even if the service's private methods are rearranged. ¶ A test that only expects a particular sequence of private method calls can fail after a harmless refactor while missing a business error. Prefer checks of required results and state. Keep integration checks for the real boundaries: request encoding, transactions, timeouts, and resource cleanup. A substitute gateway helps test orchestration but cannot establish the actual provider's behaviour. For example, testing local code with a canned timeout cannot establish whether a real provider recognises repeated operation identifiers. Choose evidence appropriate to the claim being checked. || Fake returns decline / Service records declined outcome / No shipment is initiated / Real adapter has separate contract checks

Before reorganising a large payment class, capture its important behaviours: successful payment, decline, timeout, and storage failure. In particular, keep an uncertain provider outcome distinct from a definite decline. Refactoring changes internal structure while preserving the required observable behaviour. ¶ Extract fee calculation first and compare its results on the same inputs. Then extract provider communication while keeping result categories unchanged. Run the behavioural checks after each move. Small steps make it easier to locate a mistake because fewer decisions have changed at once. ¶ A long method, duplicated rule, or repeated conditional is a code smell: a sign that deserves investigation. It is not proof of a defect or an instruction to add a design pattern. Ask what change is difficult and which responsibility or dependency causes that difficulty before choosing a repair. ¶ Tests focused on outcomes allow code to move without preserving every old private call. If the existing behaviour is wrong, changing it is a separate behavioural change that needs an explicit explanation and checks. For example, correcting a timeout that was formerly reported as decline is necessary work, but it should not be hidden inside a claim that the refactor preserves behaviour. || Characterize current outcomes / Extract one responsibility / Re-run behavioral checks / Compare failures before and after

We can express payment decisions using functions as well as objects. A pure function decide(state, command) can return a new state, events describing a decision, or requests for external actions. Another part of the application carries out any database write or provider call. This arrangement is often called a functional core with an imperative shell: calculations at the centre, effect-performing code around them. ¶ For example, given a pending payment and a recorded provider timeout, a decision function can return a pending state and a request to check the provider later. It makes no remote call itself. An outer coordinator records and performs the requested work, then supplies the observed result to the next decision. Inputs and outputs expose the reasoning without hiding communication inside it. ¶ Because the decision function has explicit inputs and no hidden effects, it is easier to test and replay. Replaying its output must not blindly repeat external actions. The effect-performing part still needs durable progress, ownership, authorisation, and duplicate handling. Calling a pure decision function twice is different from charging a provider twice. Choose functions and objects according to which makes the rules and collaboration easiest to see; they can be used together in the same design. || State plus command / Pure decision produces events / Effect interpreter executes actions / Durable owner reconciles outcomes''')

put(6, '''Suppose our notification service calls an email provider's software library, or SDK. To send a message, the service builds that library's request object and interprets its status codes. Its code depends on the SDK: a change to the provider's types can require a change to the service. ¶ We can give the application its own Sender contract, with an operation such as send(message). A provider adapter implements that operation by translating the application's message into an SDK request and translating the response back. The notification service now uses the Sender contract; the adapter knows both the contract and the provider's details. This arrangement is called dependency inversion because the application policy no longer has to depend directly on the external implementation. ¶ Distinguish two kinds of arrow in the diagram. A source dependency says which interface a piece of code must know. A runtime call says which object executes next. At runtime, the service still calls the concrete adapter, which reaches the provider. Reversing the source dependency has not reversed that call. Check the values and errors crossing the interface too: provider-specific meanings can keep the policy dependent on the SDK even after its import moves elsewhere. || Notification policy / Depends on Sender contract / Email adapter implements contract / Runtime call reaches concrete adapter

In a small script, our main function decides when to call send_receipt(). In a web application, we usually register a request handler and let the framework decide when to invoke it. We provide part of the behaviour while another part of the program controls when that behaviour runs. This is Inversion of Control, or IoC. ¶ An event loop provides another example. We register a callback; when the relevant event is ready, the loop invokes it. The callback still contains application decisions, but it no longer owns the surrounding dispatch loop. This division lets the framework provide shared connection handling and scheduling without copying those mechanisms into every handler. ¶ To use this arrangement correctly, learn when the framework considers a callback finished, how it reports errors, and what happens to work started by the callback. These rules determine where cleanup belongs. A framework may also construct handlers and supply their dependencies. Dependency Injection is one technique used in such arrangements; IoC is the broader idea of handing control of part of the execution to a coordinator. || Application registers callback / Framework owns dispatch / Framework invokes handler / Handler owns business behavior

Our notification service needs a sender. One version creates EmailSender inside its constructor. Every service instance then makes that construction choice for itself. In another version, startup code first creates sender = EmailSender(config) and then creates NotificationService(sender). Supplying a needed collaborator from outside is Dependency Injection, or DI. ¶ During a notification operation, the service calls the supplied sender's send(message) method. It does not have to choose a provider or load its configuration at that point. In a test, we can create RecordingSender and pass it to the same constructor. The test sender records the requested messages so we can inspect them; the service needs no special testing branch. ¶ Constructor arguments, function arguments, and ordinary assignment can supply dependencies. A DI container is optional. What matters is that the receiver gets the collaborators it needs through a clear contract. Passing a large infrastructure object containing dozens of unrelated services still leaves the receiver with a broad dependency. Supplying a focused Sender makes both its needs and the permitted interaction easier to see. || Construct EmailSender / Pass sender into service / Service invokes send(message) / Test substitutes RecordingSender

A notification service cannot send anything without a sender. Constructor injection supplies that required collaborator when the service is created. Once construction succeeds, callers can expect the service to have its sender. This helps prevent an object from appearing usable while a necessary field is still missing. ¶ Property or setter injection supplies a collaborator after construction. For example, a service might accept an optional observer through set_observer(). Its contract must say what happens before the observer is supplied and whether replacing it during use is allowed. If a setter supplies a mandatory dependency, some additional rule must prevent calls while the object is incomplete. ¶ Method injection supplies a collaborator for one operation, such as render(message, formatter). The receiver can use the formatter for that call without retaining it afterward. This is useful when the choice varies by call or the dependency has a shorter lifetime than the receiver. Choose the form by asking when the dependency becomes necessary and how long the receiver may keep it. A long-lived service can use a request's database session during an allowed call, but must not store it for use after that request ends. || Constructor: mandatory sender / Setter: optional observer / Method: one-call formatter / Lifetime must match actual ownership

Consider NotificationService(provider_sdk). Another part of the program supplies the SDK, so the service uses dependency injection. Yet its methods may still build provider-specific requests and catch provider-specific exceptions. Switching providers still requires changing the notification policy's code. ¶ Dependency injection answers how a collaborator reaches an object. The dependency inversion principle answers a different question: which abstractions should important policy depend on? We can define an application-owned Sender contract with the message and outcomes the policy needs. The provider adapter then converts between this contract and the SDK. Both application policy and adapter refer to the application contract, while provider details stay in the adapter. ¶ The two ideas often cooperate. Startup code can construct the adapter and inject it as a Sender. But the presence of a constructor argument alone does not establish dependency inversion. Inspect the request values, result meanings, exceptions, and resource handles that cross the boundary. A manually assembled application can respect the principle, and an application using a DI container can still depend heavily on provider details. || Concrete SDK injected / Source still depends on SDK semantics / Introduce application-owned protocol / Injection and inversion now cooperate

Before the notification application handles requests, it needs actual objects. Startup code can create a repository from a database path, create an email sender from provider configuration, and pass both to NotificationService(repository, sender). This is the composition root: the place where we choose concrete implementations and connect them into a working application. ¶ The service can then concentrate on the notification operation. It receives ready collaborators instead of deciding how to build them each time. Reading the composition root tells us which repository and sender this application uses and which objects they share. A request or background job may also have a defined boundary where its shorter-lived collaborators are assembled. ¶ A DI container automates some of this work. We register how to obtain each contract and how long its objects should be reused. When asked for a service, the container follows those registrations to construct its dependencies. For a large set of relationships, this can reduce repeated assembly code. It also makes registration and lifetime rules part of the design. Validate them early, define who releases resources, and keep broad resolution near the assembly boundary. For a few collaborators, explicit construction may be easier to understand. || Startup selects implementations / Container or code constructs graph / Validate graph and ownership / Application uses resolved collaborators

Suppose two parts of one request need the same database session. They should receive one shared session for that request, and a different request should receive a different one. A scoped lifetime provides this reuse within a defined boundary. If request one receives A twice and request two receives B twice, the identities are A,A and B,B. ¶ With a transient lifetime, each resolution creates a new instance, so two resolutions can produce C and D even within one request. With a singleton lifetime, resolutions reuse one instance within the container or application boundary stated by the framework. Both requests may therefore receive S. These rules describe when instances are created and reused; the relevant boundary must always be stated. ¶ A second process can have its own singleton. Also, a shared object may receive concurrent calls, so its implementation must support the permitted concurrent use. The word singleton supplies no such safety guarantee. Frameworks differ in registration, scope, and disposal rules. Check those rules before choosing a lifetime, and ensure that any resource the object retains remains valid for every use. || Scope 1: scoped A then A / Scope 2: scoped B then B / Transient: C then D / Singleton: S in both scopes

A web request opens a scope, obtains a database session, and creates a background job. The response ends, so the request scope closes its session. If the job retained that session, its next query uses a resource whose lifetime has already ended. Keeping a reference did not keep the session valid. ¶ Give the job its own lifetime. The request saves the necessary job data and passes durable identifiers, such as a notification identifier, to the background worker. When the worker runs, it opens a job scope, obtains a current database session, loads the data, performs the work, and closes its scope. Resource use now fits the operation that owns it. ¶ A request scope normally ends with one request. A session scope may span several requests, and a custom scope can cover a job or another explicit activity. Longer scopes also retain objects and possibly user data for longer, which affects memory and privacy. A scope describes ownership and reuse; it does not automatically start a database transaction. Nor does retaining user context establish that the user's permissions are still current when later work executes. || HTTP request opens scope R / Response ends and disposes R / Background job opens its own J / Job closes J after work finishes

A factory returns a database connection. Before using it, the receiver needs to know whether it owns the connection or is borrowing it. An owner is responsible for releasing a resource at the agreed boundary. A borrower may use it under the contract but must not close a resource that other components still need. Creation and ownership can belong to different parts of the program. ¶ For example, a factory may create a new stream and transfer ownership to its caller: the caller must close it. Another factory may return an application-owned shared sender: the caller must leave it open. Both return an object, but the responsibilities are different. Writing the ownership rule into the contract avoids both leaks and premature closure. ¶ Returned values can also depend on resources indirectly. A repository's lazy query result fetches rows as the caller reads it, so it may still need an open connection. Closing the repository before traversal ends can make the next read fail. For a bounded result, the repository can fetch the records before returning and release the connection. For a stream, return an explicit resource whose owner knows when and how to close it. || Factory creates resource / Owner retains disposal responsibility / Borrower uses within lifetime / Owner closes exactly at boundary

Assume a pool has ten connections. A request borrows one, encounters an error, and exits without returning it. Nine connections remain available. If this happens repeatedly, healthy requests eventually cannot obtain a connection even though the database itself is working. Cleanup preserves the application's capacity as well as releasing individual resources. ¶ Arrange acquisition and release together. A structured resource scope or finally-style block runs cleanup when an operation succeeds, fails, or is cancelled. Depending on the resource, cleanup may return a connection to its pool, close a stream, release a concurrency permit, or roll back an unfinished transaction. The owner should perform this work at its declared boundary. ¶ Memory garbage collection reclaims memory according to the runtime's rules. It does not guarantee that a scarce external resource will be released in time for the next request. If cleanup itself fails while another error is already being handled, preserve the original problem and record the additional failure. Otherwise, the error that caused the operation to fail may disappear behind an error from its cleanup path. || Acquire connection / Execute operation / Error or cancellation occurs / Finally returns or closes resource

A report generator needs an expensive template compilation, but many application runs never request a report. We can postpone construction until the first report request. This is lazy initialisation. It avoids unused startup work, while moving the construction delay and any construction failure to the first caller. ¶ The application needs a controlled first-use sequence. A caller discovers that the generator is absent, constructs it, and makes the completed object available to later callers. If two first callers arrive together, an uncoordinated check can let both construct it. Publishing a reference before construction finishes can let another caller use an incomplete object. Use the runtime's supported coordination and safe-publication mechanism to enforce the intended behaviour. ¶ Define failure too. Should later callers retry construction, or receive the stored failure? Can an unsuccessful attempt leave resources that need closing? For a resource essential to ordinary requests, constructing it before advertising readiness may give clearer behaviour. Lazy creation changes when construction occurs; ownership, cleanup, and safe concurrent use still need their usual rules. || First caller requests resource / Initialize once under coordination / Publish fully initialized value / Define retry after initialization failure

Suppose NotificationService needs DeliveryPlanner during construction, while DeliveryPlanner needs NotificationService. We cannot finish either constructor first. This is a dependency cycle. Examine which responsibility requires each link: shared work may belong in a third collaborator, or a separate coordinator may be responsible for the sequence. A global lookup can delay the failure until execution while leaving the underlying relationship unclear. ¶ A different problem appears when construction succeeds but lifetimes do not fit. A singleton service stores a request-scoped unit of work. The request ends and disposes that unit of work, while the singleton remains available to later requests. Its next operation uses an expired dependency. This is a captive dependency: a longer-lived object retains a dependency intended for a shorter lifetime. ¶ The operation can instead receive a currently valid collaborator for the call, or use a narrow factory that opens an appropriate scope and defines cleanup. The chosen scope must also carry the correct user and tenant context. Container validation can detect some invalid relationships, but the requirement is broader: every actual use must occur while the dependency and its context are valid. || Singleton captures request object / Request ends and disposes object / Later caller uses invalid dependency / Pass a scope-safe operation instead

Compare NotificationService(sender, repository) with a service whose send method calls global_registry.resolve(Sender). The constructor tells us what the first service needs before it runs. For the second, we must inspect its methods and the registry configuration to discover its dependencies. Such runtime lookup is called a service locator. ¶ A missing constructor argument is apparent when we assemble the service. A missing locator registration may remain hidden until a particular branch executes. Tests of that branch may also need global registry setup, and one test's changes to that registry can affect another. Explicit collaborators make these requirements easier to assemble and inspect. ¶ A locator can have a deliberate role at a framework integration boundary. Resolve the needed collaborators there or at the relevant scope boundary, then pass their focused contracts into application code. Merely injecting the entire container does not make those needs explicit if methods still request arbitrary services from it. The design question is what the receiver is allowed to obtain, when it obtains it, and who owns the resulting resources. || Explicit constructor lists dependencies / Locator defers discovery to runtime / Missing registration fails later / Resolve near composition boundary

If every notification uses email, startup code can create one email sender and inject it into the service. Now suppose each notification chooses email or SMS. The appropriate choice is not known until the command arrives. We can inject a SenderFactory with an operation that obtains the sender for that channel. ¶ During the operation, the service reads the command's channel and calls the factory. The factory selects or constructs the concrete adapter and returns it under the Sender contract. DI supplied the factory to the service; the factory makes a creation or selection decision later. They can therefore be parts of the same design. ¶ The factory must explain what it returns. Is this a fresh sender the caller must close, a shared application-owned sender, or an object borrowed from the current scope? That rule determines what the service may retain and dispose. The factory can hide provider configuration while still exposing meaningful construction failures and ownership. A narrow factory for a real runtime choice is easier to review than permission to resolve arbitrary objects from a whole container. || Inject channel factory / Select SMS for this command / Factory returns defined-lifetime sender / Caller honors ownership contract

A direct constructor is often enough to create a valid object. More varied construction can raise separate questions: who chooses the concrete writer, how do we obtain a compatible renderer and template, or how do we validate several optional notification settings? Creational patterns provide names for arrangements that answer these questions. ¶ Factory Method varies a creation operation; Abstract Factory creates a compatible family; Builder assembles a value in stages; Prototype copies a configured instance; Singleton restricts instance access within a stated boundary. Their individual treatments below follow the actual creation steps. Choose according to the variation needed, while keeping the resulting object's validity and ownership clear. || Need valid object construction / Choose variation or assembly mechanism / Establish ownership and invariants / Avoid unnecessary global state

Our notification service may need to translate a provider's interface, add timing around send(), or control who can read a result. Each change places an object between collaborators, but each has a different purpose. Structural patterns describe useful ways to arrange these objects. ¶ Adapter translates interfaces, Bridge separates two independent kinds of variation, and Composite treats leaves and groups through a common operation. Decorator adds behaviour, Facade offers a focused entry to a subsystem, Flyweight shares suitable immutable data, and Proxy controls use of a target. In the examples below, follow what each arrangement changes and what its contract preserves. A similar wrapper shape alone does not make two patterns interchangeable. || Existing collaborator contract / Translate, compose, share or guard / Preserve stated behavior / Account for added calls and state

Choosing a pricing rule, reacting to a change, and advancing a payment's lifecycle are different kinds of behaviour. Behavioural patterns help place those decisions and interactions where they can be understood and changed. ¶ Strategy supplies a selected algorithm, Observer distributes reactions, Command represents a requested action, and State organises lifecycle-dependent behaviour. Chain of Responsibility arranges handlers, while Template Method fixes a process with selected extension points. Iterator controls traversal; Mediator coordinates collaborators; Memento supports local restoration; Visitor supplies operations over known node types. The individual examples explain these relationships. Use them where the actual source of variation warrants the structure; a direct function or small conditional may already express a stable case clearly. || Behavior varies / Identify the axis of variation / Choose an explicit coordination contract / Compare with direct functions and conditionals

A reservation operation must find data, decide whether a change is allowed, and save related changes. A Repository gives the application data operations. A Specification names a rule such as eligibility. A Unit of Work places related local changes under one commit decision. CQRS separates models for commands and queries when their needs differ. ¶ The following examples explain each boundary in more detail. These roles can all exist inside one process and database. A Unit of Work still needs rollback and cleanup, and its local commit cannot atomically commit an unrelated provider's remote effect. CQRS also does not require event sourcing or separately deployed services. Choose the boundaries from the behaviour and data guarantees the application actually needs. || Repository exposes domain access / Unit of Work owns commit / Specification expresses predicate / CQRS separates model responsibilities''')

put(7, '''A shopping request arrives through a controller, invokes an application operation, applies pricing rules, and stores the order. Layered architecture gives these different kinds of work clear places: presentation, application coordination, domain policy, and infrastructure. ¶ The layers can all run in one process. Deploying them as separate physical tiers is another choice. Use the division to protect useful rules: a controller should not bypass order validation simply because it can reach an SQL helper. Follow a change, such as a new pricing rule, to see whether the boundary helps. Classes that only forward every call add work without necessarily hiding any detail. || Controller parses input / Application coordinates use case / Domain enforces rule / Infrastructure implements persistence

Catalog, orders, and payments can be modules of one deployable application. Each exposes operations and owns its data, while calls between them remain local. This is a modular monolith. ¶ The word “modular” matters. If every module writes every table, changing a payment record can still break the whole program. Use agreed interfaces and enforce dependency rules through review or useful automated checks. This arrangement keeps deployment and local transactions relatively simple while giving business responsibilities separate homes. Later extraction remains possible, but it is an option supported by the boundaries, rather than a benefit obtained automatically by naming folders. || One deployment / Catalog owns product representation / Orders owns purchase lifecycle / Payments owns payment outcomes

Suppose inventory needs to scale independently and has a team ready to operate it. Making it a separate service may provide a useful deployment and ownership boundary. It also replaces some local calls with network calls that can time out or partially fail. ¶ Microservices aim for useful independent deployment and operation. A monolith can be well organised, while several services can remain tightly coupled. If five services must release together for each change, ask how much independence was gained. Compare the motivation for extraction with the added work in data coordination, compatibility, monitoring, and recovery. A process boundary should solve a demonstrated problem. || Modular application / Identify independent operating need / Extract one stable boundary / Accept network and consistency costs

An order rule should be testable without constructing an HTTP response or starting a web framework. Clean Architecture supports this by making framework and storage details depend on interfaces used by the application's policy. ¶ The inward arrows in its usual diagrams describe source dependencies. During execution, a use case can still call an outer storage adapter through an injected interface. Avoid making core rules depend on ORM-specific records or transport response objects. The separation costs mappings and contracts, so choose a proportionate version for a small application. The important question is whether policy can be understood and exercised without unrelated infrastructure details. || Domain policy at center / Use case depends on port / Adapter implements port / Runtime call crosses outward through contract

An order can be created from a web request or a command-line tool. Both adapters can invoke the same application operation. Likewise, an SQL adapter and a test repository can satisfy the application's storage needs. ¶ Hexagonal architecture calls these application-facing contracts ports and their implementations adapters. An inbound port expresses an operation the application offers; an outbound port expresses work it needs from elsewhere. Design ports around those needs rather than copying an entire vendor API. The drawing does not imply separate machines. Test application decisions with controlled adapters, then separately check real encoding, transactions, and failure behaviour at the external boundaries. || HTTP adapter to inbound port / CLI adapter to same port / Use case invokes outbound port / SQL adapter implements persistence

Onion Architecture arranges domain policy, application services, and infrastructure so that dependencies point toward the core rules. An order rule can then use an application-owned repository contract rather than import database-specific code. ¶ Its vocabulary overlaps with Clean and Hexagonal Architecture, and teams draw the layers differently. Compare the actual dependency and ownership rules instead of treating the shape as a guarantee. Can pricing run without a web server? Does a failed write remain visible to the caller? These questions reveal whether the separation helps. Extra layers are worthwhile when they protect such properties, rather than simply matching a diagram. || Domain rule / Application coordination / Infrastructure implementation / Dependencies preserve policy independence

Ask a shop's merchandising and warehouse staff what “available stock” means. Merchandising may mean stock that can be advertised. A reservation worker may mean units that can still be promised to a buyer. Treating both as one unexamined field can produce wrong decisions. ¶ Domain-Driven Design develops a model with the people who understand the business. The model names concepts and makes their rules explicit. Use this effort where business meaning is complex or disputed. A simple lookup table may need only straightforward data handling. DDD's value comes from clearer decisions and shared understanding, rather than assigning a class to every database row. || Talk through a business scenario / Identify ambiguous terms / Name rules and invariants / Model the behavior together

Catalog's Product may describe a title, images, and marketing categories. Fulfilment's Item may describe a physical unit and its shelf location. Both models can be correct for their own work. ¶ A bounded context states where a model and its vocabulary apply. Within it, a ubiquitous language gives team members and code consistent names for the business concepts. Between contexts, translate deliberately and identify who owns each fact. One universal Product schema may obscure real differences. A context is a modelling boundary; deciding whether it becomes a separate service, repository, or team requires further reasons. || Catalog Product model / Translation boundary / Fulfillment Item model / Each context owns its meaning

An order's line items and total must agree when it is committed. We can give an Order root responsibility for changing those related values together. An aggregate is this kind of consistency boundary, with a root controlling changes to its members. ¶ Entities have identity through change; value objects are distinguished by their values. A domain service can express a rule that does not belong to one entity. Keep an aggregate focused on invariants that need a common atomic change. Putting the entire shop into one aggregate creates contention and loading costs. Rules spanning aggregates need an explicit coordination design; an object reference alone does not create a distributed transaction. || Command reaches aggregate root / Root validates local invariant / Members change atomically / Cross-aggregate work needs a protocol

After an order is accepted, other parts of the system may need to know. OrderPlaced records a business fact. Give it a stable identity, clear time meaning, and information that its intended consumers can interpret across versions. ¶ Creating an event object in memory is not the same as publishing it reliably. The process may commit the order and die before sending the event. An outbox records order state and publication intent in one local transaction, then relays the event. Consumers still need to handle repeated and delayed delivery. Define a fact's meaning before deciding how the broker will carry it. || Order transition commits / OrderPlaced intent persisted / Relay publishes event / Consumers deduplicate and interpret version

Suppose the orders module updates a payment table directly. It now knows the payment representation and can bypass payment rules. A payment interface gives that interaction a controlled place. ¶ Dependency rules specify which modules may know about or call others. Enforce important boundaries with appropriate import checks, database permissions, or architecture tests. A cycle can reveal that coordination has no clear owner. Shared helpers also need scrutiny: one “common model” can accidentally force unrelated contexts to change together. Where meanings genuinely differ, use an explicit translation rather than sharing a type merely because its fields currently look similar. || Orders uses payment contract / Payments owns payment tables / Direct table write is rejected / Boundary checked in source and storage

“A refunded order must not ship” is a business rule. Asking the payment service for a refund and then updating progress is orchestration. Encoding and sending a provider request is infrastructure work. ¶ These responsibilities may begin in one function, but distinguishing them helps assign tests and handle failures. Test the shipping rule as a decision. Test orchestration across success and partial failure. Test the provider adapter against its actual protocol. Moving a sequence onto a queue does not make its business rules belong to the broker. Keep the owner of each decision visible even when the execution arrangement changes. || Policy decides allowed transition / Orchestrator requests effects / Adapter communicates externally / Result returns to explicit state machine

A controller reads a request and builds a response. An application service coordinates an operation. A repository offers data operations, and an adapter connects a contract to a particular protocol or technology. ¶ These are roles, not required class-name endings. A service can be a function; a domain service is not necessarily a network service. Examine the work the code performs. If a controller calculates prices and retries transactions, creating an empty “services” directory has not moved those responsibilities. Choose names and boundaries that help readers follow one real operation from its input to its result. || Transport data to command / Application use case / Domain-oriented data access / Boundary-specific implementation

A legacy payment provider may use “settled” for an authorisation that our application still considers pending. Copying that label directly would import the provider's meaning into our own model. ¶ An anti-corruption layer translates the external model into the local one. It maps known states, retains useful provider references, and handles unknown meanings explicitly. This adds mapping and maintenance work, but keeps external quirks from spreading through business code. Never turn an unmapped result silently into success. Where the provider's state is uncertain or different from ours, preserve enough information to investigate and reconcile it. || Legacy provider state / Explicit semantic translation / Local payment state / Unknown state remains an actionable error

An accepted order can trigger a receipt without making checkout wait for email delivery. Event-driven architecture allows components to react independently to recorded events. This can remove a synchronous dependency from the user's path. ¶ Decide which steps may actually be delayed. Inventory and payment requirements may require stronger coordination than receipts. Event processing also adds delivery, ordering, replay, and monitoring work. Name the owner of each event and state how it is persisted, recognised on redelivery, and recovered after a failure. A broker transports messages; the application must still arrange for the required business outcome. || Durable order event / Broker stores or routes message / Consumer updates its state / Reconciliation detects incomplete outcomes

A cloud function can process an uploaded file when an event arrives. The platform manages invocation and some execution capacity, but the function still has deadlines, concurrency limits, credentials, and possible retries. ¶ Treat function-local memory as temporary unless the platform guarantees otherwise. Store required progress durably, and recognise repeated invocations before applying repeated effects. Small independently triggered tasks can fit this arrangement well. A long sequence with human review or remote uncertainty needs a durable owner for its state, often a workflow engine. “Serverless” changes operating responsibilities; it leaves application correctness and recovery decisions to be designed. || Event invokes function / Function receives bounded execution / External state persists outcome / Retry may invoke another instance

The application must keep tenant data isolated while releases change its internals. A check that attempts a cross-tenant read can protect that property during development. Similar checks can protect module boundaries, migration compatibility, or a latency budget. ¶ Evolutionary architecture uses such feedback to change a system while preserving important qualities. A fitness check should express a useful property, rather than freeze an incidental class layout. Record why a choice exists and when evidence should prompt reconsideration. Evolution may involve removing an unnecessary service or simplifying a contract. It is a process of guided change, not an obligation to replace the architecture repeatedly. || Change is proposed / Fitness checks test required properties / Evidence supports a reversible step / Decision rationale evolves with workload

Controllers and database adapters are technical groupings. Orders and fulfilment are business capabilities. The two kinds of division can coexist: each business module can have its own internal input, policy, and storage code. ¶ Making separate network services for “validation” and “SQL” may force every business operation to call both. Begin with ownership of decisions and facts, then choose the technical arrangement inside those boundaries. A bounded context describes a model's scope. It does not automatically dictate a deployment or team structure. Follow actual business changes to see which parts need to evolve together. || Business capability owns decisions / Internal layers implement mechanics / External contract hides representation / Deployment follows an actual need

Five services share writable tables and must be released together. Most requests synchronously pass through all five. Although deployed separately, they cannot change or fail independently. This is commonly called a distributed monolith. ¶ Network boundaries now add timeouts and debugging work without delivering the expected independence. Clarify data ownership and contracts before promising independent services. Sometimes consolidating unstable boundaries into a modular application is the easier improvement. In other cases, redesigning the interactions can justify the separation. Judge progress by independent behaviour and manageable failures, rather than the number of deployed processes. || Several services share schema / Every release deploys all services / One dependency stalls the chain / Clarify ownership or consolidate boundary

A small team is still discovering how orders and fulfilment should work. Splitting them into many services immediately would add contract and operating work while the boundaries are changing. A modular monolith can provide a simpler starting point. ¶ Consider extraction when a stable capability has distinct scaling, isolation, or ownership needs and the team can operate it. Include distributed transactions, version compatibility, telemetry, security, and incident response in the cost. Neither low traffic nor a service count settles the choice. Prefer the design that meets the actual requirements and can be changed and recovered by the people responsible for it. || Small team and changing model / Modular deployment satisfies workload / Measure concrete scaling or ownership pain / Extract only a justified capability''')

put(8, '''A client calls create-payment. It needs to know the amount units, currency, permitted caller, returned states, and what to do if no reply arrives. A field schema alone cannot tell it whether retrying might charge twice. ¶ An API is an interface together with a communication contract. Describe inputs, results, effects, errors, timing, operation identity, and supported evolution. Begin with concrete histories: successful creation, invalid input, a repeated request, and a lost reply after submission. These expose decisions a transport framework cannot make for you. Keep a definite provider decline distinct from an operation whose outcome is still uncertain. || Client sends payment intent / Server validates identity and shape / Operation has explicit side effect / Result defines retry and uncertainty

The same payment capability can be exposed in several ways. REST emphasises resources and HTTP semantics. GraphQL lets clients query a typed graph. gRPC commonly supplies schema-defined remote calls and streaming. RPC more generally models operations called across a boundary. ¶ Choose by what clients need, how the API will evolve, and the cost of serving it. GraphQL can reduce unwanted data transfer but needs query-cost and field-access controls. An RPC name can make an action clear while leaving retries and compatibility to be specified. Compare complete operation histories and tooling, rather than assuming a request syntax establishes reliability or performance. || Same payment business operation / Resource API or operation API / Schema and authorization obligations / Transport does not decide correctness

A payment and its refund are related, but they have different identities and lifecycles. A refund may be requested later, authorised separately, and succeed or fail independently. ¶ One resource model uses POST /payments, GET /payments/{id}, and POST /payments/{id}/refunds. The refund then has its own amount, status, and operation identifier. Resource modelling gives these continuing business objects clear identities. Avoid a generic /execute endpoint that hides every meaning, while also avoiding a forced CRUD model that makes domain actions unclear. Let ownership and allowed state transitions guide the interface. || Payment resource ID / Independent refund resource ID / Refund references original payment / Both have explicit status transitions

A request can contain valid JSON yet ask to charge a negative amount. Parsing establishes structural validity; application validation checks business meaning. Check both. ¶ Bound body size, collection length, and supported encodings before expensive work. Decide how unknown fields are handled so clients can evolve predictably. Return a stable machine-readable error code with a useful, safe message and a request identifier. Avoid exposing secrets or raw provider internals. Consider strict clients as well as tolerant ones: a new field can affect a client that rejects unknown input. The schema and its compatibility policy belong together. || Bound request bytes / Parse declared schema / Validate business invariant / Return stable safe error model

The server returns 201 after creating a payment resource whose status is pending. The resource exists; money has not necessarily been captured. Similarly, 202 indicates acceptance for processing rather than final approval. ¶ HTTP status describes the protocol response, while the body can describe the business operation's state. A timeout supplies neither a definitive approval nor a decline. Use application error codes to distinguish invalid input, conflicts, denied access, and uncertain submitted work. This lets clients choose an appropriate next action. Turning every provider problem into the same generic error can hide whether the caller should correct input, wait, or query an existing operation. || HTTP request accepted / Payment resource is pending / Later business outcome arrives / Client uses status endpoint to reconcile

A request for every payment can return too much data and consume too much memory. Pagination divides the collection into bounded responses. Define maximum page size, permitted filters, ordering, and access checks. ¶ Sorting only by creation time can leave rows with equal timestamps in an unstable order. Add a unique tie-breaker such as the payment identifier. Decide what happens while the collection changes between page requests. Neither offset nor cursor pagination automatically gives the client one frozen snapshot. Enforce tenant access in the query itself, rather than retrieving a large shared collection and hoping the client filters it correctly. || Filter authorized records / Sort by time then unique ID / Apply bounded page size / Return continuation metadata

Suppose a client reads the first ten rows, then asks to skip ten. A new row inserted near the beginning shifts positions, so the second page may repeat or omit an item. This is a difficulty with offset pagination on changing data. ¶ A keyset cursor instead records the last ordered key, such as (created_at,id), and continues after that key. It can reduce skipping cost and these shifting-position problems. Updates to existing rows still need a stated consistency policy. Validate opaque cursors or protect their integrity, and bind them to the applicable filters and tenant. A cursor must not become a way to escape the original query's access rules. || Page ends at key (100,42) / New row inserted before that key / Continue after (100,42) / Cursor remains bound to query and tenant

A payment amount changes from cents to dollars while its JSON type remains numeric. Old clients can now submit a hundred times the intended amount. Compatibility concerns meaning as well as syntax. ¶ Adding an optional field is safer for tolerant clients than changing an existing field's meaning, but strict validators and enum handling may still break. Use an explicit version policy, an overlap period, and evidence about old consumers. Test representative clients. A versioned URL is one tool; it does not migrate callers or update stored events. Plan the transition for every reader that still relies on the earlier contract. || Existing client contract / Proposed semantic change / Compatibility test and migration window / Retire old behavior with evidence

A client retries a payment after losing its reply. It sends the same idempotency key so the server can recognise the original logical operation. The server must bind that key to the authenticated owner, request content, progress, and result. ¶ Enforce uniqueness and the required atomic updates in storage. If the same key arrives with a different amount, return a conflict instead of replaying an unrelated result. Define how long the key is remembered. A provider timeout may require querying or reconciling that provider before another submission. Local key storage alone cannot promise one external charge if the provider does not support the necessary operation identity and recovery behaviour. || Key K and payload hash H / Atomically reserve operation K / Execute or reconcile provider operation / Persist and replay the same result

A user signs in successfully, then requests payment 42. The server still needs to check whether that user owns or may view payment 42. Knowing an identity and granting an action are separate decisions. ¶ Authentication establishes identity under the chosen trust mechanism. Authorisation checks permission for this action on this resource. Derive tenant context from validated membership rather than trusting an arbitrary tenant_id field. A gateway can perform coarse checks, but the service with ownership information must enforce the resource rule. Apply appropriate identity checks to internal service callers too. An obscure identifier reduces guessing; it is not an access-control mechanism. || Validate credential / Resolve permitted tenant and roles / Load resource under tenant constraint / Authorize the requested action

A client makes too many expensive validation requests at once. A rate limit controls that pace, often with a specified burst allowance. A daily export quota instead caps accumulated use over a longer period or resource dimension. ¶ State the identity being limited, window, cost unit, retry guidance, and behaviour if the limiter fails. Per-tenant controls can protect neighbours from one noisy client. Per-user limits alone may miss coordinated abuse. Across regions, a strict global limit needs shared coordination or safely allocated local budgets. If enforcement is approximate, specify the approximation rather than silently giving every region the full global allowance. || Identify tenant and operation / Check burst and sustained allowance / Admit or reject with retry guidance / Account for distributed enforcement scope

A payment request has an overall deadline. Connection acquisition, each attempt, and any backoff must fit inside it. Passing only a fresh full timeout to every downstream call can make the total wait exceed the client's intended limit. ¶ Retry transient work only where repetition is safe. Exponential backoff with jitter spreads attempts but does not add capacity. If four layers each allow three attempts, one intent can cause up to 3⁴ = 81 downstream attempts. Choose a shared retry budget and propagate remaining time. Side effects additionally need a stable operation identity and a way to resolve outcomes that may already have committed. || Parent deadline established / Attempt consumes remaining time / Backoff includes jitter / Stop when budget or safe retry condition ends

A report takes minutes to generate. Instead of holding the initial request open, the server can return an operation resource that the client polls or subscribes to. Required progress must be stored beyond the request's memory. ¶ A streaming API returns a sequence of messages and needs framing, buffer limits, cancellation, and possibly a resume policy. Decide whether disconnecting cancels the business job or only the connection. A slow reader should not accidentally hold an unbounded database transaction. Use bounded pages or an explicitly owned streaming resource. The interface must explain how work is tracked after the initiating client has gone away. || Submit operation / Persist job and return operation ID / Worker advances durable state / Client observes or cancels under contract

A payment service sends a webhook announcing a completed operation. The receiver commits its local effect, but the success reply is lost. The sender retries, so the same event arrives again. ¶ Give webhooks stable event identifiers and a documented signature and timestamp-verification policy. The receiver should persist the required inbox or effect before acknowledging, then recognise redelivery. The sender needs bounded retries and a recovery policy. A 2xx reply establishes only what the receiver's contract promises; it does not mean every later downstream action is complete. Treat the acknowledgment as a named step in the delivery protocol. || Sender signs event E / Receiver verifies and stores E / Receiver acknowledges receipt / Lost acknowledgment causes duplicate delivery

A restored consumer replays last year's events. It encounters fields and status values no current request uses. Deleting the old decoder can therefore break recovery even if today's API traffic works. ¶ Schema evolution applies to events and stored records as well as live requests. Preserve field meanings, defaults, version identity, and handling of unknown values. Test historical samples and any transformations used to read them. An extra field can also affect signatures or uniqueness rules, so “additive” does not automatically mean harmless. Plan compatibility for the full life of the data, including replay, backup restoration, and delayed clients. || Historical event version 1 / New decoder supports old meaning / Version 2 adds explicit field / Replay validates both histories

A consumer expects a payment status to be either pending or final. A provider test that checks only “status is a string” misses that assumption. Consumer-driven contracts record what actual clients need from their provider. ¶ Run relevant provider checks before releasing a change, including representative errors and state behaviour. Keep the contracts focused on meaningful obligations rather than incidental JSON formatting. Schema tests alone cannot establish retry safety, authorisation, or pagination correctness. Use concrete histories for these behaviours. The contract is useful when it protects a real dependency while still allowing the provider to change internal implementation details. || Consumer states required behavior / Provider verifies contract / Release checks compatible semantics / Runtime telemetry catches unmodeled behavior

A mobile screen needs three small pieces of data from several services. A Backend for Frontend, or BFF, can assemble the response around that client's needs. A gateway usually handles shared edge concerns, such as routing and coarse admission. ¶ Both add operating work and can become bottlenecks. Keep ownership checks in services that know the resource rules, and give the aggregation layer a clear owner. A universal BFF that every product must edit can reduce independence. If results are cached, define the key, tenant scope, and freshness. Aggregating private results does not make them safe for a shared public cache. || Mobile client requests screen data / BFF aggregates bounded calls / Gateway applies edge policy / Domain services enforce ownership

Two internal services may release at different times and communicate over a network. Their API therefore still has compatibility, trust, load, and failure concerns. “Internal” does not mean that every request is valid or authorised. ¶ A public API usually needs longer compatibility commitments, discoverability, abuse protection, and support processes. State the intended audience and lifetime of both kinds. Internal interfaces can become important dependencies whose accidental behaviour is difficult to change. Document their meaningful guarantees early, and apply proportionate authentication, validation, and resource limits instead of relying solely on location inside a network. || Identify API audience / Define compatibility duration / Enforce identity and capacity policy / Observe consumers before changing behavior

A developer needs to create a payment and recover if the reply is lost. Documentation that shows only a successful request leaves the most consequential decision unexplained. ¶ Include complete examples for validation errors, access denial, pagination, operation keys, timeouts, and pending states. A machine-readable schema can generate client types, while prose explains the behaviour those types cannot express. Keep examples aligned with the implementation and test the important ones. A sandbox must clearly distinguish simulated effects from real charges. Good discoverability means a reader can find the operation, understand its scope, and choose a supported recovery action when the usual sequence is interrupted. || Find operation / Read schema and behavioral contract / Try safe example / Follow documented recovery path''')
